Before we train Dataset A, I want us to do these checks:

Freeze the exact six class definitions. No ambiguous small_device definitions once preprocessing starts.
Deduplicate before splitting. Exact + source groups + perceptual near-duplicate checks. UNU has already shown why this is mandatory.
Ignore source-provided splits. After combining all sources, create one new group-aware split.
Check source bias within every class. A class should not be identifiable from “green cloth”, “black bars”, “white lab background”, etc.
Use annotation-based padded crops where available. Probably around 10–20% surrounding margin initially. Not extremely tight crops.
Make inference match training. Your app's camera guide should tell the worker to photograph one complete item, centered and reasonably large. Then the deployment distribution resembles our padded-crop training distribution.
Flag extreme images instead of blindly using everything. Tiny, heavily clipped, questionable label, bizarre aspect ratio, etc.
Audit cleaned samples manually. Before training, randomly inspect roughly 30–50 cleaned images from every class and every source.
Balance classes AND sources. We won't simply use all 2,828 batteries against ~800 phones because “more data = better”.
Collect the real-world test set before final training, not afterward. Around 100–300 staged phone-camera images across the six classes, with real tables, hands, clutter, lighting and different devices. These never enter training. This is our early warning system for domain shift.
Run a baseline before doing fancy modelling. If a simple pretrained classifier performs badly, we diagnose the data first rather than immediately changing architectures.
Keep augmentation after splitting only. That prevents augmented relatives leaking into validation/test.